g"],"author":[{"given":"Naga Sekhar","family":"Madala","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"UAB 1119 11th Pl S, Apt 213,AI Data Science Cybersecurity scientist Department of Computer Science,Birmingham,Alabama,USA,35205"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"A.","family":"Muniyappan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Chennai Institute of Technology,Centre for Nonlinear Systems,Chennai,Tamil Nadu,India,600 069"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/icdsaai69492.2026.11505153","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,26]]},"container-title":["2026 International Conference on Data Science, Agents and Artificial Intelligence (ICDSAAI)"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-3-030-93789-8_67-2","title":["Transactive Memory System"],"author":[{"given":"Mar\u00eda del Pilar","family":"Garc\u00eda-Chitiva","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-3-030-93789-8_67-2","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2024,4,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"container-title":["The Palgrave Encyclopedia of Memory Studies"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/icdsaai65575.2025.11011853","title":["Bidirectional Long Term Short Memory Based Text Mining Classification Method for Analysing the What\u2019s App Messages with Text and Emoji"],"author":[{"given":"M.","family":"Divya","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Erode Arts and Science College,Erode,Tamilnadu"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"S.","family":"Sukumaran","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Erode Arts and Science College,Erode,Tamilnadu"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/icdsaai65575.2025.11011853","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,28]]},"container-title":["2025 International Conference on Data Science, Agents &amp;amp; Artificial Intelligence (ICDSAAI)"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5044854","title":["NPD Team Conflicts and Team Learning: The Mediating Effect of the Transactive Memory System"],"author":[{"given":"claret  thedory","family":"mwalongo","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5044854","issued":{"date-parts":[[2024]]}},{"DOI":"10.1201\/9781003599067-10","title":["AI Computing with Semiconductor Memory (In-Memory Computing)"],"author":[{"given":"Takayuki","family":"Kawahara","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1201\/9781003599067-10","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,31]]},"container-title":["Hardware Technologies for Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.heliyon.2025.e42209","title":["Retraction notice to \u201cExploring the transactive relationships of influence factors for online asynchronous learning transactive memory system\u201d [Heliyon 10 (2024) e36441]"],"author":[{"given":"Jiao","family":"Zhang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Min","family":"Zhao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Liang","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.heliyon.2025.e42209","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,2]]},"container-title":["Heliyon"]},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,30]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Human memory is undergoing a significant reconfiguration in the age of artificial intelligence. As Bratton suggests and Kornbluh implies, memory has already been transformed into a stack. Building on these insights, this essay addresses the dangers of turning memory into consumable content and of allowing AI technologies to govern the circulation of digitalized memories. The concern is that memory will soon take the form of memory-capital. This study examines how the link between experience and memory is being effaced, and how AI reshapes the ways in which memory is produced, stored, shared, represented, and ultimately returned to the subject. AI accelerates this process of capitalization and the pursuit of immediacy in memory. The essay reflects on the risk of delinking\u2014the potential dissociation of presence from absence, and experience from memory\u2014resulting from the growing dominance of AI over human remembrance, a condition that further obscures human agency in the production and reproduction of memory.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.19116\/theory.2025.30.3.219","title":["Memory as a Stack: On the Reconfiguration of Memory in the Age of Artificial Intelligence"],"author":[{"name":"The Criticism and Theory Society of Korea","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"JeeHee","family":"Kim","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,30]]},"container-title":["The Criticism and Theory Society of Korea"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,30]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.19116\/theory.2025.30.3.219"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-3-032-15112-4_46","title":["Decision Procedures for Artificial Moral Agents"],"author":[{"given":"Tyler","family":"Cook","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-3-032-15112-4_46","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["The Palgrave Handbook on the Ethics of Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1515\/9783112223062-fm","title":["Frontmatter"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1515\/9783112223062-fm","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,1]]},"container-title":["Agentic Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.7551\/mitpress\/15999.003.0015","title":["Grounded, Embodied, and Situated Intelligence in Humans and Artificial Agents"],"author":[{"given":"Carlotta I.","family":"Zona","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Martin H.","family":"Fischer","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Alex","family":"Miklashevsky","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7551\/mitpress\/15999.003.0015","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,23]]},"container-title":["Embodied Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1201\/9781003599067-2","title":["Overview of Artificial Intelligence Hardware LSI (AI Chips) and Its Components"],"author":[{"given":"Takayuki","family":"Kawahara","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1201\/9781003599067-2","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,31]]},"container-title":["Hardware Technologies for Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.3389\/frai.2026.1805539","title":["Artificial intelligence agents as advanced decision support systems in public decision-making: evidence from Peru"],"author":[{"given":"Emilio J.","family":"Medrano-S\u00e1nchez","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Engineering","place":["Universidad Tecnol\u00f3gica del Per\u00fa, Peru"]}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Jadira","family":"Jara","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Graduate School","place":["Universidad Tecnol\u00f3gica del Per\u00fa, Peru"]}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3389\/frai.2026.1805539","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,13]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>\n                    Decision making in the public sector faces persistent challenges that have driven the analysis of technologies aimed at strengthening institutional performance. Within this context, the present study examined the association between the valuation of artificial intelligence agents (AIA) and decision making among public officials with national-level responsibilities in Peru. In this study, AIA are conceptualized as advanced decision support systems (DSS) that complement human judgment in complex public-sector decisions. A Likert-scale survey was administered to 93 participants and showed high internal consistency (Cronbach's alpha = 0.952). Given the non-parametric nature of the data, associations were estimated using Kendall's Tau-b and Spearman's Rho. The results revealed a positive and statistically significant association between the valuation of AIA and overall decision-making (Tau-b = 0.588; Rho = 0.653;\n                    <jats:italic>p<\/jats:italic>\n                    &amp;lt; 0.001). Complementarily, the observed pattern was consistent with the notion that higher valuation of AIA is linked to more favorable perceptions of the decision-making process across the analyzed dimensions. In conclusion, the findings support that, in the Peruvian context, the valuation of AIA is associated with decision making perceived as more effective, suggesting their relevance as a complementary support to human judgment and as an AI-based DSS to guide evidence-based modernization of public management.\n                  <\/jats:p>","container-title":["Frontiers in Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1201\/9781003082880-19","title":["Agents"],"author":[{"given":"Alan","family":"Dix","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Janet","family":"Finlay","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1201\/9781003082880-19","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,6,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,6,6]]},"container-title":["Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.engappai.2025.110083","title":["Dual replay memory reinforcement learning framework for minority attack detection"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-9291-7313","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ankit","family":"Sharma","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6979-090X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Manjeet","family":"Singh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.engappai.2025.110083","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,3]]},"container-title":["Engineering Applications of Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/rev-ai70456.2026.11622132","title":["Visual Memory-Augmented Architecture for Persistent Multimodal Conversational Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-6058-3326","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Lokanatha Reddy","family":"Gandikota","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-0376-225X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Venkata Raghavendra Swamy","family":"Gudipati","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Remington College","place":["Lafayette, USA"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-9721-0962","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Sudheer Kumar","family":"Aluvala","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"HCL Tech","place":["Prosper, USA"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-1359-9225","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Sateeshkumar","family":"Ponugoti","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Satya Tulsi Ram","family":"Konda","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/rev-ai70456.2026.11622132","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"container-title":["2026 International Conference on Revolutionary Artificial Intelligence and Future Applications (Rev-AI)"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/mitp.2025.3550697","title":["Revolutionizing Higher Education: The Impact of Artificial Intelligence Agents and Agentic Artificial Intelligence on Teaching and Operations"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-1408-4212","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Nir","family":"Kshetri","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of North Carolina at Greensboro, Greensboro, NC, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/mitp.2025.3550697","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,3]]},"container-title":["IT Professional"]},{"DOI":"10.5220\/0014492200004052","title":["Artificial Intelligence-Based Multimodal Alzheimer Prediction System (AI-MAPS): A System Overview"],"author":[{"given":"Achref","family":"Kassaoui","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"ReDCAD, ENIS, University of Sfax, Tunisia"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Imen","family":"Abdennadher","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"ReDCAD, ENIS, University of Sfax, Tunisia"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Ismael","family":"Rodriguez","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"ReDCAD, ENIS, University of Sfax, Tunisia"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5220\/0014492200004052","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 18th International Conference on Agents and Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.engappai.2023.107074","title":["Scheduling operations in a large hospital by multiple agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4237-4820","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Noam","family":"Gaon","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yuval Gabai","family":"Schlosberg","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-1410-8368","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Roie","family":"Zivan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.engappai.2023.107074","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2023,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2023,11]]},"container-title":["Engineering Applications of Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1142\/9789819815678_others02","title":["ENTREPRENEURSHIP AND PROFESSIONAL GROWTH WITH AI AGENTS"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1142\/9789819815678_others02","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,7]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,5,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,5,29]]},"container-title":["Agentic Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1515\/9783112223062-toc","title":["Contents"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1515\/9783112223062-toc","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,1]]},"container-title":["Agentic Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/tai.2024.3375834","title":["A Self-Aware Digital Memory Framework Powered by Artificial Intelligence"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5102-4200","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Prabuddha","family":"Chakraborty","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Electrical &#x0026; Computer Engineering, University of Maine, Orono, ME, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6082-6961","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Swarup","family":"Bhunia","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Electrical &#x0026; Computer Engineering, University of Florida, Gainesville, FL, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/tai.2024.3375834","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,7]]},"container-title":["IEEE Transactions on Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-10384653\/v1","title":["Financial Discourse on the First Social Network for Artificial Intelligence Agents"],"author":[{"given":"Valerio Lo","family":"Monaco","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Bergamo"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-10384653\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,20]]},"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>Artificial-intelligence agents no longer act alone, but they interact with one another at scale, coordinating, competing and building together. On Moltbook, the first large social network whose members are exclusively AI agents rather than humans, they post, build software, and trade cryptocurrency tokens in public. This paper offers the first portrait of such a community. The study apply the novel textual-factors method, a scalable topic-modelling approach, to the population of 407,321 posts written by 11,989 agents between 3 and 20 March 2026. This window brackets Meta Superintelligence Labs\u2019 acquisition of the platform, announced on 10 March. The discourse resolves into twelve clusters, dominated by debates on cryptocurrency and agent engineering. The composition turns after the acquisition. Cryptocurrency, the dominant cluster, averages 48.7% of daily posts before the announcement and 31.1% after. Within two weeks, the mix of activity moves from cryptocurrencies trading to infrastructure. This study documents the pattern and its timing, offering a novel question for research on emerging technologies and the direction of innovation: whether, and how, a change of ownership redirects what a machine community works on.<\/p>"},{"DOI":"10.1142\/9789819815678_0012","title":["SCALING AI AGENTS: FROM VISION TO REALITY"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1142\/9789819815678_0012","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,7]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,5,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,5,29]]},"container-title":["Agentic Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.63962\/mabc9179","title":["Designing Deterministic AI Agents in Enterprise Platforms: A Schema-Guided Reasoning Approach"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.63962\/mabc9179","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,20]]},"container-title":["International Conference on Artificial Intelligence Management and Trends","The International Conference on Artificial Intelligence Management and Trends"]},{"DOI":"10.24963\/ijcai.2025\/2","title":["AriGraph: Learning Knowledge Graph World Models with Episodic Memory for LLM Agents"],"author":[{"given":"Petr","family":"Anokhin","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"AIRI, Moscow, Russia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Nikita","family":"Semenov","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Skoltech, Moscow, Russia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Artyom","family":"Sorokin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"AIRI, Moscow, Russia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Dmitry","family":"Evseev","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Skoltech, Moscow, Russia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Andrey","family":"Kravchenko","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Oxford, UK"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mikhail","family":"Burtsev","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"London Institute for Mathematical Sciences, London, UK"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Evgeny","family":"Burnaev","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Skoltech, Moscow, Russia"},{"name":"AIRI, Moscow, Russia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.24963\/ijcai.2025\/2","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,9]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Advancements in the capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs) have created a promising foundation for developing autonomous agents. With the right tools, these agents could learn to solve tasks in new environments by accumulating and updating their knowledge. Current LLM-based agents process past experiences using a full history of observations, summarization, retrieval augmentation. However, these unstructured memory representations do not facilitate the reasoning and planning essential for complex decision-making. In our study, we introduce AriGraph, a novel method wherein the agent constructs and updates a memory graph that integrates semantic and episodic memories while exploring the environment. We demonstrate that our Ariadne LLM agent, consisting of the proposed memory architecture augmented with planning and decision-making, effectively handles complex tasks within interactive text game environments difficult even for human players. Results show that our approach markedly outperforms other established memory methods and strong RL baselines in a range of problems of varying complexity. Additionally, AriGraph demonstrates competitive performance compared to dedicated knowledge graph-based methods in static multi-hop question-answering.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Proceedings of the Thirty-Fourth International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/icdsaai65575.2025.11011587","title":["Advanced Real Estate Price Forecasting Using Multivariate Long Short-Term Memory Networks"],"author":[{"given":"Sabareswari","family":"T C","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Chennai Institute of Technology,Dept of Electronics and Communication Engineering,Chennai,Tamil Nadu,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Veeramalai","family":"Sankaradass","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Chennai Institute of Technology,Dept. of Computer Science and Engineering,Chennai,Tamil Nadu,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Reina Mercy","family":"Ravi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Chennai Institute of Technology,Dept. of Computer Science and Engineering,Chennai,Tamil Nadu,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"R","family":"Ramesh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Chennai Institute of Technology,Dept. of Computer Science and Engineering,Chennai,Tamil Nadu,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Poongothai","family":"P","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Chennai Institute of Technology,Dept of Mathematics,Tamil Nadu,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"R.","family":"Ponnusamy","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Chennai Institute of Technology,Dept. of Computer Science and Engineering,Chennai,Tamil Nadu,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/icdsaai65575.2025.11011587","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,28]]},"container-title":["2025 International Conference on Data Science, Agents &amp;amp; Artificial Intelligence (ICDSAAI)"]},{"DOI":"10.1613\/jair.1.19798","title":["Monitoring Teams of AI Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-8477-8079","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Korok","family":"Ray","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1613\/jair.1.19798","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,14]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Background: Generative AI agents will need to work together, which requires monitoring and managing their performance.\nObjectives: The chief objective of this paper is to understand the joint design choice of the number of agents and their rewards.\nMethods: We study this problem in a theoretical framework of optimal incentives, where a system designer (principal) selects the environment in which multiple autonomous decentralized AI agents work together. These agents respond to incentives, such as rewards and penalties. We first consider a principal who selects the size of the agent team in addition to their incentives.\nResults: We prove a general result that the optimal team size will vary with the parameters of the environment, but the optimal incentives will not. This invariance property shows that agents should have different-sized teams on work projects rather than differing financial incentives.\nConclusions: We show these results are robust to a more general framework, where the principal employs a supervisory AI agent to manage the tasks of the underlying AI team. Finally, we propose different levels of quality for the supervisory and worker agents, and find that it is efficient to match the best supervisors with the best worker agents.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research"]},{"DOI":"10.1201\/9781003599067-8","title":["Semiconductor Memory and Computing"],"author":[{"given":"Takayuki","family":"Kawahara","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1201\/9781003599067-8","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,31]]},"container-title":["Hardware Technologies for Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1201\/9781003599067-9","title":["Main Semiconductor Memory Features"],"author":[{"given":"Takayuki","family":"Kawahara","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1201\/9781003599067-9","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,31]]},"container-title":["Hardware Technologies for Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.22541\/au.174733751.13557469\/v1","title":["The Resonant Knot Memory: A Topological Model of Meaningful Retention in Artificial Intelligence"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-6080-3198","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ken","family":"Park","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.22541\/au.174733751.13557469\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,5,15]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>This paper proposes a novel model of memory in artificial intelligence, called the Resonant Knot Memory (RKM). RKM is based on a topological structure of response resonance among information units, or \"knot elements\" (phi), which allows for dynamic filtering and meaningful retention of data. Unlike standard vector-based memory or sequential token traces, RKM enables retention through rhythmic coherence, activation strength, and structural resonance. We explore the formal structure of RKM, contrast it with existing models, and outline its implications for AI systems and philosophical understandings of memory and identity.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.171995361.18726361\/v1","title":["Memory Between Bio-cell and Semiconductors: An Introduction to Hybrid Artificial Intelligence"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-3953-8166","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Prof. Hazar","family":"Shtat","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.171995361.18726361\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,7,2]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>During my time as a theoretical researcher at the quantum laboratories of the University of Paris-Sud in Orsay, France, in the late 1980s, I had the opportunity to meet one of the most prominent writers on simplifying quantum cybernetics and machine language, Mr. Viktor Davidovich Pekelis, a writer, specializing in quantum cybernetics who was visiting at the time for the Orsay Research Center (University of Paris-Sud).<\/jats:p>\n                <jats:p>It was at this exact stage that the idea for this article began, but it did not take its current final form until 2023 \/ passing through a draft that was published in the TRONIX Magazine in Arabic in 2005.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/icdsaai65575.2025.11011788","title":["Artificial Intelligence in Image Analysis For Dermatology And Dermatopathology: Advancements And Challenges"],"author":[{"given":"R.","family":"Balachander","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Chennai Institute of Technology,Department of IT,Chennai,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"G.","family":"Yohalakshmi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Chennai Institute of Technology,Department of IT,Chennai,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"A.R.","family":"Kavitha","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Chennai Institute of Technology,Department of IT,Chennai,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/icdsaai65575.2025.11011788","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,28]]},"container-title":["2025 International Conference on Data Science, Agents &amp;amp; Artificial Intelligence (ICDSAAI)"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/aaicv66571.2025.00063","title":["Teaching Strategies for Improving Memory Effect of College English Vocabulary Based on Artificial Intelligence Algorithm"],"author":[{"given":"Zhang","family":"Yuanwei","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Ordos Institute of Technology,Ordos,China,017000"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/aaicv66571.2025.00063","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,21]]},"container-title":["2025 International Conference on Algorithm, Artificial Intelligence and Computer Vision (AAICV)"]},{"DOI":"10.1080\/08839514.2024.2381165","title":["Trading Strategy of the Cryptocurrency Market Based on Deep Q-Learning Agents"],"author":[{"given":"Chester S. J.","family":"Huang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Money and Banking, National Kaohsiung University of Science and Technology, Kaohsiung City, Taiwan (Republic of China)"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yu-Sheng","family":"Su","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Computer Science and Information Engineering, National Chung Cheng University, Chiayi, Taiwan (Republic of China)"},{"name":"Department of Computer Science and Engineering, National Taiwan Ocean University, Keelung City, Taiwan (Republic of China)"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1080\/08839514.2024.2381165","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,12,31]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2024,7,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,7,21]]},"container-title":["Applied Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.3233\/faia260511","title":["LLM-Enabled Social Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6982-5658","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"\u00d6nder","family":"G\u00fcrcan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"NORCE Research AS, Universitetsveien 19, Kristiansand, Norway. E-mail: ongu@norceresearch.no"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5436-6070","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Moharram","family":"Challenger","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Antwerp and Flanders Make, Belgium. E-mail: moharram.challenger@uantwerpen.be"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3233\/faia260511","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,2]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Large Language Models (LLMs) have transformed agent\u2013agent and human\u2013agent interaction by enabling software, physical, and simulation agents to communicate and deliberate through natural language. Yet fluent language use does not by itself yield socially intelligible behaviour. Most current systems remain weakly grounded in roles, norms, intentions, and contextual constraints, limiting their capacity for meaningful participation in social environments. This paper develops a conceptual baseline for LLM-enabled social agents by arguing that they should be grounded in role definitions operationalized through persona descriptions. On this basis, we outline research directions for representation, hybrid control, and evaluation. The paper concludes that persona-based role definitions are a necessary foundation for turning language competence into social behaviour.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Frontiers in Artificial Intelligence and Applications","HHAI 2026"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.engappai.2023.106046","title":["MA-GCN: A Memory Augmented Graph Convolutional Network for traffic prediction"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0223-5447","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Dunlu","family":"Peng","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yongsheng","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.engappai.2023.106046","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2023,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2023,5]]},"container-title":["Engineering Applications of Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1142\/9789819815678_0009","title":["A PRACTICAL GUIDE FOR BUILDING SUCCESSFUL AI AGENTS"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1142\/9789819815678_0009","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,7]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,5,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,5,29]]},"container-title":["Agentic Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.5220\/0013400200003890","title":["Unified Framework for Implementing Inaccurate Knowledge in Quantum Symbolic Artificial Intelligence Models"],"author":[{"given":"Eduardo","family":"Mosqueira-Rey","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Computer Science and Information Technologies, University of Coru\u00f1a (CITIC), Campus de Elvi\u00f1a s\/n, A Coru\u00f1a, Spain"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Samuel","family":"Magaz-Romero","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Computer Science and Information Technologies, University of Coru\u00f1a (CITIC), Campus de Elvi\u00f1a s\/n, A Coru\u00f1a, Spain"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Vicente","family":"Moret-Bonillo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Computer Science and Information Technologies, University of Coru\u00f1a (CITIC), Campus de Elvi\u00f1a s\/n, A Coru\u00f1a, Spain"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5220\/0013400200003890","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 17th International Conference on Agents and Artificial Intelligence"]},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Artificial Intelligence (AI)-driven chatbots and intelligent agents are increasingly deployed to support monitoring, evaluation, and organisational learning. Advances in large language models, retrieval-augmented generation, and multi-agent architectures have expanded conversational AI. Despite growing adoption, existing research remains fragmented across technical, educational, and organisational domains, limiting holistic understanding of their design, impact, and governance. This gap creates challenges for organisations seeking evidence-based guidance for implementation. The purpose of this study is to examine and synthesise existing literature on the design, adoption, and impact of AI-based chatbots and intelligent agents within the contexts of monitoring, evaluation, and internal organisational operations. This study presents a systematic literature review and bibliometric analysis of peer-reviewed studies (2021-2025). The review analyses publication trends, core techniques, and application areas of AI-driven chatbots and intelligent agents. Findings reveal rapid growth and increasing focus on organisational learning and evaluation use cases. The study contributes a consolidated synthesis of techniques, benefits, and challenges, identifies research gaps, and offers directions for future research and evidence-based adoption of conversational AI in organisational environments.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.26697\/aids.2026.5","title":["Artificial Intelligence-Driven Chatbots and Intelligent Agents for Monitoring, Evaluation, and Organisational Learning: A Review of Techniques and Trends"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5455-7064","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"A. T.","family":"Kgopa","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4709-0763","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"N. T.","family":"Msweli","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Artificial Intelligence in Digital Society","Collective Monograph on Artificial Intelligence in Digital Society Vol 1 2026"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.26697\/aids.2026.5"},{"DOI":"10.1088\/978-0-7503-6169-9ch4","title":["Memristive devices as computational memory"],"author":[{"given":"Shaibal","family":"Mukherjee","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Indian Institute of Technology Indore","place":["India"]},{"name":"QuanTechL2M Innovations Pvt. Ltd.","place":["India"]}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1088\/978-0-7503-6169-9ch4","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,1]]},"container-title":["Resistive Switching Systems for In-Memory Computation and Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.5220\/0013149900003890","title":["A Systematic Review of Sustainable Supplier Selection Using Advanced Artificial Intelligence Methods"],"author":[{"given":"Hanen","family":"Neji","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"ReDCAD,ENIS,University of Sfax, Tunisia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mouna","family":"Rekik","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"MIRACL,ISIMS,University of Sfax, Tunisia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Lotfi","family":"Souifi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"ReDCAD,ENIS,University of Sfax, Tunisia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ismail","family":"Rodriguez","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"ReDCAD,ENIS,University of Sfax, Tunisia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5220\/0013149900003890","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 17th International Conference on Agents and Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.23899788.v1","title":["Contextualizing Climate Change Solutions Through the Deployment of Cooperative Artificial Intelligence (AI) Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7323-4380","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ignatius","family":"Ogbaga","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Ebonyi State University Abakaliki"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ezeanya","family":"Christiana","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.23899788.v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2023,8,14]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Climate change presents one of the most significant challenges facing\nhumanity in the 21st century. Mitigating its impacts and developing\nsustainable solutions requires a multidisciplinary approach that\nleverages advanced technologies. This paper proposed the deployment of\ncooperative Artificial Intelligence (AI) agents as a viable strategy for\ncontextualizing solutions to climate change. Firstly, it explored the\nuse of AI agents in analyzing climate data, predicting future trends,\nand informing decision-making processes. This data-driven approach can\nhelp policymakers, scientists, and stakeholders to better understand the\ncomplex dynamics of climate change and design effective mitigation and\nadaptation strategies. This research also addressed ethical\nconsiderations associated with the deployment of AI agents in climate\nchange contexts. It emphasized the importance of transparent and\naccountable AI systems, ensuring that their decisions align with human\nvalues and priorities. Additionally, it discussed the need for\ninterdisciplinary collaboration to guide the development and deployment\nof AI agents, fostering inclusivity, fairness, and equitable access to\nAI technologies. Finally, this paper highlighted the potential of\ncooperative AI agents as a powerful tool for contextualizing solutions\nto climate change. By harnessing their analytical capabilities,\nadaptability, and collaboration potential, these agents can facilitate\nevidence-based decision-making, optimize resource management, and\nsupport the transition to a sustainable future. However, it also\nemphasizes the importance of addressing ethical concerns and fostering\nresponsible AI development to ensure that AI agents serve as enablers\nrather than substitutes for human judgment and action.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.31224\/7439","title":["Artificial Intelligence (AI) Agents in Construction: A Conceptual Framework Across Autonomy Types and Application Domains"],"author":[{"given":"Reihaneh","family":"Samsami","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.31224\/7439","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,27]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Construction is one of the least digitized and most hazard-prone industries, and signs of risk often go unconnected to timely action, as the 2025 NIST findings on the Champlain Towers South collapse illustrate. Artificial intelligence (AI) agents have been proposed as a response, but the term spans computer vision monitors, physical robots, and large language model (LLM) reasoning systems studied in separate communities. This paper develops a conceptual framework connecting agent autonomy to the construction problem it addresses. The study is a structured review and synthesis of peer-reviewed and industry literature published mainly between 2014 and 2026, drawn from construction engineering, robotics, and computer science. The framework organizes agents along two axes: autonomy type (perceptual, cognitive, embodied) and application domain (safety monitoring, design and modeling, project and contract management, progress monitoring, on-site automation), rating the maturity of each pairing by weight of evidence. Perceptual agents are mature where the task is to see and report, such as safety and progress monitoring. Embodied agents are mature only for narrow physical tasks such as aerial survey. Cognitive agents reach every domain but are mature in none, and remain unreliable for safety-critical calculation. The most capable systems combine agent types rather than one alone. The framework provides the first two-axis view that unifies three separate agent literatures and makes explicit where construction capabilities are mature, overlapping, or absent. It gives transportation agencies a tool for matching an agent type to a construction need and prioritizing investment, and identifies three deployment requirements, namely human oversight, system integration, and trust, that practitioners must meet for AI agents to move from pilots to practice.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6536278","title":["Artificial Intelligence Agents are Responsive to Identity Cues in Persuasive Health Communication"],"author":[{"given":"Xiaoli","family":"Nan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xingman","family":"Wu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6536278","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>This paper investigates whether large language models (LLMs), such as GPTs, can effectively simulate human responses to persuasive health messages. Across two studies, AI agents were assigned political and religious identities and exposed to COVID-19 vaccine messages featuring identity-based cues. In Study 1, AI agents' responses aligned with their political affiliations, exhibiting greater persuasion by messages endorsed by politically congruent figures. In Study 2, AI agents identified as moderately or highly religious Christians responded more favorably to vaccine messages incorporating religious identity cues than to messages without such cues. These findings suggest that LLMs can simulate nuanced human-like responses to health communication, offering a valuable tool for pretesting and refining public health campaigns. Additionally, the results reinforce the persuasive power of identity-based cues, highlighting their potential as an effective strategy in health messaging. We conclude by discussing the implications of LLMs for health communication and outlining future research directions.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1038\/s41928-026-01652-x","title":["Artificial intelligence agents automate life-cycle assessment"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1038\/s41928-026-01652-x","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,17]]},"container-title":["Nature Electronics"]},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.23899788","title":["Contextualizing Climate Change Solutions Through the Deployment of Cooperative Artificial Intelligence (AI) Agents"],"author":[{"given":"Ignatius","family":"Ogbaga","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Ezeanya","family":"Christiana","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.23899788","issued":{"date-parts":[[2023,8,14]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>&lt;p&gt;Climate change presents one of the most significant challenges facing humanity in the 21st century. Mitigating its impacts and developing sustainable solutions requires a multidisciplinary approach that leverages advanced technologies. This paper proposed the deployment of cooperative Artificial Intelligence (AI) agents as a viable strategy for contextualizing solutions to climate change. Firstly, it explored the use of AI agents in analyzing climate data, predicting future trends, and informing decision-making processes. This data-driven approach can help policymakers, scientists, and stakeholders to better understand the complex dynamics of climate change and design effective mitigation and adaptation strategies. This research also addressed ethical considerations associated with the deployment of AI agents in climate change contexts. It emphasized the importance of transparent and accountable AI systems, ensuring that their decisions align with human values and priorities. Additionally, it discussed the need for interdisciplinary collaboration to guide the development and deployment of AI agents, fostering inclusivity, fairness, and equitable access to AI technologies. Finally, this paper highlighted the potential of cooperative AI agents as a powerful tool for contextualizing solutions to climate change. By harnessing their analytical capabilities, adaptability, and collaboration potential, these agents can facilitate evidence-based decision-making, optimize resource management, and support the transition to a sustainable future. However, it also emphasizes the importance of addressing ethical concerns and fostering responsible AI development to ensure that AI agents serve as enablers rather than substitutes for human judgment and action.\u00a0&lt;\/p&gt;<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1515\/9783112223062-203","title":["VIIContributing Authors"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1515\/9783112223062-203","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,1]]},"container-title":["Agentic Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.5220\/0013160300003890","title":["Stateful Monitoring and Responsible Deployment of AI Agents"],"author":[{"given":"Debmalya","family":"Biswas","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Wipro AI, Switzerland"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5220\/0013160300003890","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 17th International Conference on Agents and Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.caeai.2025.100473","title":["Tailoring AI agents for early learning: The Creative Project Approach"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8057-2863","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Weipeng","family":"Yang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Hui","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"John Chi-Kin","family":"Lee","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.caeai.2025.100473","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12]]},"container-title":["Computers and Education: Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.caeai.2026.100576","title":["Designing conversational Agents for adaptive instructional support in business simulation gaming"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3953-1277","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Anna","family":"Wenzel","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jan-Martin","family":"Geiger","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Andreas","family":"Liening","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.caeai.2026.100576","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"container-title":["Computers and Education: Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.engappai.2024.108295","title":["Predictive process model monitoring using long short-term memory networks"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0389-0275","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Johannes","family":"De Smedt","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6151-0504","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jochen","family":"De Weerdt","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.engappai.2024.108295","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,7]]},"container-title":["Engineering Applications of Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1080\/08839514.2024.2385854","title":["Fraud Detection Based on Credit Review Texts with Dual Channel Memory Networks"],"author":[{"given":"Yansong","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Science and Technology Innovation Center, Chery Huiyin Motor Finance Service Co, Ltd, Wuhu, China"},{"name":"School of Data Science, University of Science and Technology of China, Hefei, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Defu","family":"Lian","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Data Science, University of Science and Technology of China, Hefei, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Enhong","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Data Science, University of Science and Technology of China, Hefei, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1080\/08839514.2024.2385854","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,12,31]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2024,8,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,8,27]]},"container-title":["Applied Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s44202-026-00697-5","title":["Artificial intelligence literacy moderating the effects of artificial intelligence use on cognitive outcomes including memory retention decision making and cognitive laziness"],"author":[{"given":"Muhammad","family":"Tanveer","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s44202-026-00697-5","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,18]]},"container-title":["Discover Psychology"]},{"DOI":"10.51483\/ijaiml.6.2s.2026.781-787","title":["Adaptive Memory Retrieval Algorithms for Long-Term Context Retention in Autonomous Agents"],"author":[{"given":"Dr. Shanthi","family":"Vairavan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Dr.S.U","family":"Aswathy","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"K","family":"Anitha","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Dr.E.P","family":"John","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Dr.C","family":"Rajan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Dr. Anupa","family":"Sinha","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.51483\/ijaiml.6.2s.2026.781-787","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,12]]},"container-title":["International Journal of Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning"]},{"DOI":"10.1609\/aaai.v40i25.39205","title":["MemoryART: Enhancing LLMs via Multi-Memory Models with Adaptive Resonance Theory for Healthcare Agents"],"author":[{"given":"Renke","family":"Dai","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Hebin","family":"Hu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Jiahui","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yilin","family":"Kang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Ah-Hwee","family":"Tan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1609\/aaai.v40i25.39205","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,14]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Though promising in healthcare consultation applications, large language models (LLMs) face critical limitations in retaining and utilizing long-term memory across multi-turn interactions. In particular, existing memory enhancing paradigms are constrained by limited context windows and embedding-based retrieval, often failing to maintain task relevance and still suffering from memory prototype collapse in multi-turn healthcare consultation. To address these challenges, we propose a cognitively-inspired memory framework named MemoryART, which is grounded in Adaptive Resonance Theory (ART)\u2014a cognitive and learning theory of how humans and animals adapt to dynamic environments. MemoryART employs three memory modules\u2014working memory, episodic memory, and semantic memory to support task-aware memory organization and dynamic retrieval. Specifically, episodic memory provides the storage of specific experiences along with contextual clues, which is crucial for managing patient-specific information and perfect for multi-turn healthcare consultation interactions. Building upon this concept, MemoryART leverages multi-channel competitive learning and resonance matching to enable efficient and interpretable episodic memory encoding, alleviating issues of prototype collapse and noisy memory associations. For evaluation, we construct a long-term medical dialogue benchmark called MediLongChat using a LLM-based generation pipeline. The resulting dataset features realistic, multi-disease chat histories, each exceeding 100K tokens across 20\u201330 dialogues, simulating real-world healthcare interaction patterns. Our experimental results show that MemoryART outperforms mainstream approaches in memory-intensive tasks, achieving SOTA results and significantly reducing token consumption across five popular LLMs, confirming its effectiveness and efficiency in providing scalable, reliable memory for LLMs in healthcare.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3811238.3812684","title":["FedMBRL: Federated Memory Bank Reinforcement Learning for Multi-Agent Edge Caching in Industrial IoT"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-7068-8883","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yi","family":"Tian","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Economics and Management, Tianjin University of Technology and Education, Tianjin, Tianjin, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-2834-1208","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Wentao","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Computer and Information Engineering, Tianjin Normal University, Tianjin, Tianjin, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-6601-1418","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Rui","family":"Lan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Computer and Information Engineering, Tianjin Normal University, Tianjin, Tianjin, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-5809-197X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zhihao","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Electronic and Communication Engineering, Tianjin Normal University, Tianjin, Tianjin, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5205-6167","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jia","family":"Guo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Computer and Information Engineering, Tianjin Normal University, Tianjin, Tianjin, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0295-3468","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Chenyang","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Computer Science and Software Engineering, Shenzhen University, Shenzhen, Guang Dong, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2362-1304","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Weijia","family":"Feng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Computer and Information Engineering, Tianjin Normal University, Tianjin, Tianjin, China and JingWei Hi-Tech (Tianjin) Co., Ltd., Tianjin, Tianjin, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3811238.3812684","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,27]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,27]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 2026 International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Agents"]},{"DOI":"10.1201\/9781003599067","title":["Hardware Technologies for Artificial Intelligence"],"author":[{"given":"Takayuki","family":"Kawahara","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1201\/9781003599067","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,31]]}},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.23586063.v1","title":["Augmented Reality-Based Human Memory Enhancement Using Artificial Intelligence"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-9366-7047","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zhanat","family":"Makhataeva","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Nazarbayev University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-1712-1269","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Tolegen","family":"Akhmetov","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"H\u00fcseyin Atakan","family":"Varol","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.23586063.v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2023,7,8]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Augmented reality (AR) offers novel ways to design, curate, and deliver\ninformation to users by integrating virtual, computer-generated objects\ninto a real-world environment. This work presents an AR-based human\nmemory augmentation system that uses computer vision (CV) and artificial\nintelligence (AI) to replace the internal mental representation of\nobjects in the environment with an external augmented representation.\nThe system consists of two components: (1) an AR headset and (2) a\ncomputing station. The AR headset runs an application that senses the\nindoor environment, sends data to the computing station for processing,\nreceives the processed data, and updates the external representation of\nobjects using a virtual 3D object projected into the real environment in\nfront of the user\u2019s eyes. The computing station performs computer\nvision-based indoor environment self-localization, object detection, and\nobject-to-location binding using first-person view (FPV) data received\nfrom the AR headset.<\/jats:p>\n                <jats:p>We designed a behavioral study to evaluate the usability of the system.\nIn a pilot study with 26 participants (12 females and 14 males), we\ninvestigated human performance in an experimental task that involved\nremembering the positions of objects in a physical space and displaying\nthe positions of the learned objects on the two-dimensional (2D) map of\nthe space. We conducted the studies under two conditions\u2014that is, with\nand without using the AR system. We investigated the usability of the\nsystem, subjective workload, and performance variables under both\nconditions. The results showed that the AR-based augmentation of the\nmental representation of objects indoors reduced cognitive load and\nincreased performance accuracy.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1142\/9789819815678_0013","title":["CASE STUDY AND USE CASES OF AGENTS ACROSS INDUSTRIES"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1142\/9789819815678_0013","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,7]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,5,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,5,29]]},"container-title":["Agentic Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.23586063","title":["Augmented Reality-Based Human Memory Enhancement Using Artificial Intelligence"],"author":[{"given":"Zhanat","family":"Makhataeva","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-1712-1269","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Tolegen","family":"Akhmetov","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"H\u00fcseyin Atakan","family":"Varol","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.23586063","issued":{"date-parts":[[2023,7,8]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>&lt;p&gt;Augmented reality (AR) offers novel ways to design, curate, and deliver information to users by integrating virtual, computer-generated objects into a real-world environment. This work presents an AR-based human memory augmentation system that uses computer vision (CV) and artificial intelligence (AI) to replace the internal mental representation of objects in the environment with an external augmented representation. The system consists of two components: (1) an AR headset and (2) a computing station. The AR headset runs an application that senses the indoor environment, sends data to the computing station for processing, receives the processed data, and updates the external representation of objects using a virtual 3D object projected into the real environment in front of the user's eyes. The computing station performs computer vision-based indoor environment self-localization, object detection, and object-to-location binding using first-person view (FPV) data received from the AR headset.&lt;\/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;We designed a behavioral study to evaluate the usability of the system. In a pilot study with 26 participants (12 females and 14 males), we investigated human performance in an experimental task that involved remembering the positions of objects in a physical space and displaying the positions of the learned objects on the two-dimensional (2D) map of the space. We conducted the studies under two conditions---that is, with and without using the AR system. We investigated the usability of the system, subjective workload, and performance variables under both conditions. The results showed that the AR-based augmentation of the mental representation of objects indoors reduced cognitive load and increased performance accuracy.&lt;\/p&gt;<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.5220\/0011635300003393","title":["Teleo-Reactive Agents in a Simulation Platform"],"author":[{"given":"Vasileios","family":"Apostolidis-Afentoulis","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Applied Informatics, School of Information Sciences, University of Macedonia, 156 Egnatia Str., GR-54636 Thessaloniki, Greece, --- Select a Country ---"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Ilias","family":"Sakellariou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Applied Informatics, School of Information Sciences, University of Macedonia, 156 Egnatia Str., GR-54636 Thessaloniki, Greece, --- Select a Country ---"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5220\/0011635300003393","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2023]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2023]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 15th International Conference on Agents and Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.174803777.75571709\/v1","title":["StudSar: A Neural Associative Memory System for Artificial Intelligence"],"author":[{"given":"Francesco","family":"Bulla","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-0466-4718","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Stephanie","family":"Ewelu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Satya Praveen","family":"Yalla","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.174803777.75571709\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,5,23]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>StudSar is a novel neural associative memory system engineered to emulate human-like mechanisms for forming, storing, and retrieving memories in artificial intelligence (AI), addressing critical limitations in existing memory models. Inspired by human learning strategies, StudSar processes extensive textual data through a structured workflow that segments inputs into semantically rich blocks, generating 384-dimensional embeddings using the 'all-MiniLM-L6-v2' model from the Sentence Transformers library. These embeddings serve as associative markers, enabling real-time knowledge integration and precise, similarity-based retrieval via a custom StudSarNeural network implemented in PyTorch. Through iterative refinements, StudSar has evolved to incorporate advanced features, including dynamic memory updates, enhanced contextual handling, and metadata integration, such as emotional tags (e.g., \"curiosity\"), reputation scores, and usage frequency; mimicking human memory dynamics where frequently accessed information is reinforced. Unlike conventional AI assistants, which struggle to accurately link to specific fragments within large inputs, particularly as data scales, StudSar excels at pinpointing exact information with context-aware precision, even in expansive corpora. This paper elucidates StudSar's architecture, detailing its five-stage pipeline: text segmentation, embedding generation, marker creation, network integration, and query-driven retrieval. Experimental results demonstrate robust retrieval accuracy (e.g., cosine similarity scores of 0.6652-0.7981), persistent memory across sessions, and adaptability to new data, validated through tests on diverse queries and metadata-driven scenarios. StudSar's scalability and modular design position it as a transformative contribution to next-generation AI systems, with applications in conversational agents, personalized learning platforms, and knowledge management. By bridging intuitive human memory processes with technical innovation, StudSar lays a foundation for advanced cognitive features, such as emotional state modeling and memory consolidation, paving the way for AI systems that more closely emulate human intelligence. This work offers a comprehensive analysis of StudSar's implementation and potential, highlighting its role in advancing AI memory research.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.5220\/0012342100003636","title":["Decentralized Intelligence for Smart Agriculture"],"author":[{"given":"Pascal","family":"Faye","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Universit\u00e9 du Sine Saloum El Hadj Ibrahima NIASS (USSEIN), Sing Sing, Kaolack, Senegal, --- Select a Country ---"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jeanne","family":"Faye","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Universit\u00e9 Aix-Marseille, Institut de Management Public et Gouvernance Territoriale, Aix-Marseille, France, --- Select a Country ---"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mariane","family":"Senghor","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Universit\u00e9 du Sine Saloum El Hadj Ibrahima NIASS (USSEIN), Sing Sing, Kaolack, Senegal, --- Select a Country ---"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5220\/0012342100003636","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 16th International Conference on Agents and Artificial Intelligence"]},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,6,10]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>This research identifies ethical considerations and regulatory policies for deploying fully autonomous financial artificial intelligence agents within the financial services sector. Ethical considerations for human supervision are examined within the context of the use of AI within the financial sector. Specifically, does the autonomous and semi-autonomous use in the financial services sector of algorithms, machine learning processes, neuro-fuzzy systems, and deep learning present new ethical themes? It is followed by a consideration of regulatory uncertainty and regulatory gaps and how these will hinder the movement for some industry participants toward the use of fully autonomous AI agents. The paper then draws brief conclusions and indicates the necessity of further research in these areas.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.70593\/978-93-49910-62-1_12","title":["Addressing ethical challenges and regulatory gaps in deploying fully autonomous financial artificial intelligence agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-6556-7848","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Bharath","family":"Somu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,6,10]]},"container-title":["The Future of Financial IT: Agentic Artificial Intelligence and Intelligent Infrastructure in Modern Banking"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,6,10]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.70593\/978-93-49910-62-1_12"},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.engappai.2023.106478","title":["Rational software agents with the BDI reasoning model for Cyber\u2013Physical Systems"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7262-992X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Burak","family":"Karaduman","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4873-7848","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Baris Tekin","family":"Tezel","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5436-6070","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Moharram","family":"Challenger","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.engappai.2023.106478","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2023,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2023,8]]},"container-title":["Engineering Applications of Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1201\/9781003460299-12","title":["Other Universal Agents"],"author":[{"given":"Marcus","family":"Hutter","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"David","family":"Quarel","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Elliot","family":"Catt","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1201\/9781003460299-12","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,4,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,4,30]]},"container-title":["An Introduction to Universal Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.5220\/0014295800004052","title":["Automatic Detection and Remediation of Faults in Wi-Fi Networks: An Artificial Intelligence-Based Approach"],"author":[{"given":"Raul","family":"Frandes","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Mathematics and Computer Science, Babes,-Bolyai University, Cluj-Napoca, Romania"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Ioan","family":"Pop","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Mathematics and Computer Science, Babes,-Bolyai University, Cluj-Napoca, Romania"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5220\/0014295800004052","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 18th International Conference on Agents and Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.4397618","title":["Reactance to Human versus Artificial Intelligence: Why Positive and Negative Information from Human and Artificial Agents Leads to Different Responses"],"author":[{"given":"Cindy","family":"Candrian","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Anne","family":"Scherer","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.4397618","issued":{"date-parts":[[2023]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>People increasingly receive information from artificial agents, such as robo-advisors and artificial intelligence, be it on retirement planning, health insurance or social benefits. Sometimes, this information is negative and undesirable for the person receiving it. Little research has examined the interplay of who is giving the information and what information is provided. Across two experiments and one field study, we show that people accept positive information more readily than negative information when the source is a human agent, while they are equally accepting of both when the source is an artificial agent. Drawing from reactance theory, we show that these differences arise because threatening information arouses less reactance when provided by a non-social rather than a social source, which, in turn, reduces reactance-style responses. These results have important implications for organizations in the public and private sector that need to confront people with information they may not like to hear.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.4337\/9781035336906.00009","title":["AI agents"],"author":[{"given":"Franklin","family":"Graves","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4337\/9781035336906.00009","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,12]]},"container-title":["Elgar Concise Encyclopedia of Artificial Intelligence and the Law"]},{"DOI":"10.1163\/29498902-202400019","title":["Is Artificial Intelligence the Future of Collective Memory?"],"author":[{"given":"Sarah","family":"Gensburger","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Sociology, CNRS \/ Sciences Po, Paris, France"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Fr\u00e9d\u00e9ric","family":"Clavert","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"History, C2DH, University of Luxembourg, Esch-sur-Alzette, Luxembourg"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1163\/29498902-202400019","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,12,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,12,10]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>\n                    This\n                    <jats:italic>Memory Studies Review<\/jats:italic>\n                    special issue explores the intricate relationship between artificial intelligence (\n                    <jats:sc>ai<\/jats:sc>\n                    ) and collective memory. In the one hand, the emergence of generative\n                    <jats:sc>ai<\/jats:sc>\n                    , exemplified by ChatGPT\u2019s 2022 release, appears to herald a new infrastructure for collective memory. On the other, the memory studies work highlights the limits and the backlashes of this new form of memory in its social dimension. This leads to raise a provocative, open-ended question: Is artificial intelligence the future of collective memory? Our issue brings together diverse perspectives from memory studies scholars of different backgrounds and machine learning practitioners, fostering critical engagement with\n                    <jats:sc>ai<\/jats:sc>\n                    in memory practices. This multidisciplinary approach offers an initial exploration of the interactions between\n                    <jats:sc>ai<\/jats:sc>\n                    -powered software, platforms, and collective memory. The articles herein present a multifaceted analysis of\n                    <jats:sc>ai<\/jats:sc>\n                    \u2019s role in shaping collective memory\u2019s future. We advocate for increased interdisciplinary collaboration and ethical reflection in this rapidly evolving domain, providing memory studies scholars with a foundation for understanding and engaging with these technological transformations.\n                  <\/jats:p>","container-title":["Memory Studies Review"]},{"DOI":"10.4018\/979-8-3373-2277-3.ch004","title":["Artificial Intelligence Agents Powered by Synthetic Data"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7821-3685","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Sachin","family":"Sharma","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Amity University, Mohali, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4018\/979-8-3373-2277-3.ch004","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,7,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,7,25]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The merging of Artificial Intelligence (AI) agentry and progressed built yields talks to a transformative paradigm in how cleverly systems work and associated with the world. AI masters, characterized by their independence, considering, organizing, and learning capabilities, are dynamically being utilized to perform complex errands with irrelevant human intercession. These specialists as often as possible require endless sums of data for planning, endorsement, and operation. This collaboration addresses some fundamental challenges. Firstly, designed data can overcome the obstacles of real-world data, such as deficiency, inclination, assurance concerns, and the tall fetched of procurement and comment. By producing data that accurately reflects the genuine properties of veritable data, This capability opens unused streets for amusement, testing, and the creation of novel advanced substance.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Digital Synthetic Data and Outputs"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-3-031-91084-5_8","title":["AI Agents: From Perception to Action"],"author":[{"given":"Rajendra","family":"Akerkar","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-3-031-91084-5_8","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.dss.2025.114562","title":["Artificial intelligence agents or human agents? Impact of online customer service agents on crowdfunding performance"],"author":[{"given":"Wei","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yao","family":"Tong","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jian","family":"Mou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.dss.2025.114562","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,1]]},"container-title":["Decision Support Systems"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6899418","title":["Warrant Clearing for Artificial Intelligence Agents: Maintaining Justified Claims Across Recursive Agentic Synthesis"],"author":[{"given":"Jabran","family":"Chaudry","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Anthony","family":"Bowen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6899418","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Agentic systems recursively synthesize claims and reuse those claims as premises for further reasoning and action. In current systems, the justification supporting each claim is rarely preserved as a structured, queryable object across steps. This creates epistemic debt-reasoning chains whose total support, contradictions, and conditions of validity become progressively more difficult to inspect. The problem originates at two layers: first, at the knowledge-creation and indexing layer, where claims enter AI-accessible systems without adequate structural preparation; and second, at the agentic reasoning layer, where inadequately structured claims are synthesized and reused without justification tracking. This paper introduces Warrant Clearing as a protocol for maintaining Justified Claims across recursive agentic synthesis, and integrates the Knowledge Structuring Model (KSM\u2122; Bowen, 2026) as the upstream architectural framework that improves the quality of knowledge entering the protocol. A Justified Claim is a signed object that carries a claim, direct evidence, explicit contradictions, action constraints, adjudication status, and justification history. The Justification Adjudication Protocol requires contradictions to be surfaced and justification history updated at each synthesis or reuse step before a claim may be reused for further reasoning or action. The framework is also complementary to the Model Context Protocol (MCP): MCP standardizes access to tools and external context, while Warrant Clearing preserves the justification of claims derived from that context as they propagate through recursive synthesis. When agents reason over self-referential histories, the protocol uses declared bounds and records residual uncertainty explicitly. Together, KSM\u2122, MCP, and Warrant Clearing describe a layered architecture for epistemic integrity in artificial intelligence agents:<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.engappai.2026.114897","title":["On-device generative anomaly detection for unmanned aerial vehicle telemetry using memristor analog compute-in-memory"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7450-5728","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Hyojung","family":"Ahn","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.engappai.2026.114897","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,8]]},"container-title":["Engineering Applications of Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1142\/9789819815678_0003","title":["THE FIVE LEVELS OF AI AGENTS: FROM AUTOMATION TO AUTONOMY"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1142\/9789819815678_0003","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,7]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,5,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,5,29]]},"container-title":["Agentic Artificial Intelligence"]},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,6,6]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>What if an AI chatbot could operate a task entirely on its own in software and\/or hardware? What if, when you asked your favorite AI chatbot to schedule a meeting with someone, it created your calendar appointment, contacted the person, negotiated the time slot, and sent reminders, handling any situation that arose, entirely without your further input? These so-called \"autonomous\" or \"agentic\" AI models are rapidly emerging. In this chapter, we explain how autonomous AI systems already exist now and provide an overview of what they can do. We also discuss the implications this new AI paradigm could have for work and decision-making (Bryson et al., 2020; Acharya et al., 2025; Hughes et al., 2025).\n\nWe frame agentic AIs as general-purpose computing systems that are able to perform an open-ended set of tasks in various software and hardware environments on behalf of or rather than just in support of human users. Central to our conceptualization of autonomous agents are three properties: the ability to act (and finalize tasks) on behalf of users rather than just in support of users; the ability to execute a wide variety of different tasks that are not limited to query-response or dialog functions; and the ability to work and\/or interact in the physical world as well as in virtual environments. We define Agentic AI as those technologies and applications that are based on Large Language Models and their variants that exhibit the properties of such autonomous agents. Commercial \"Agentic AI\" systems and apps utilize also other AI-centric approaches including multimodal or evolutionary learning and\/or hybrid models that integrate LLMs with neural-symbolic, cognitive architecture, or common sense reasoning components. Initial projects in the relatively newly emerging field of Agentic AI focus either on using LLMs to augment existing automation technology or on autonomous process optimization and decision-making. But fully enabling Agentic AI would involve a significant redesign of current processes to fit into an autonomous model that assigns agencies to both humans and agents, a design that allows agents to perform their task autonomously with assistance from human users only when necessary and that allows human users to monitor agent operations and interventions at low cost (Stone et al., 2016; Russell &amp; Norvig, 2020; Sapkota et al., 2025).<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.70593\/978-81-988918-1-5_3","title":["Understanding agentic artificial intelligence: Autonomous digital agents and their impact on workflows and decisions"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-6728-945X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Abhishek","family":"Dodda","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,6,6]]},"container-title":["Artificial Intelligence and Financial Transformation: Unlocking the Power of Fintech, Predictive Analytics, and Public Governance in the Next Era of Economic Intelligence"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,6,6]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.70593\/978-81-988918-1-5_3"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/icdsaai69492.2026.11505079","title":["Smart Notes Artificial Intelligence: Summaries, Flashcards and Advanced Study"],"author":[{"given":"G","family":"Senthil Kumar","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Chennai Institute of Technology,Department Of Computer Science And Business Systems,Chennai,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Balaraman","family":"Sundarambal","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Chennai Institute of Technology,Department Of Computer Science And Business Systems,Chennai,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"N","family":"Kirubakaran","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Chennai Institute of Technology,Department Of Computer Science And Business Systems,Chennai,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"M","family":"Vaishnavee","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Chennai Institute of Technology,Department Of Computer Science And Business Systems,Chennai,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"R","family":"Nithyashree","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Chennai Institute of Technology,Department Of Computer Science And Business Systems,Chennai,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"R","family":"Sivakuomar","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Chennai Institute of Technology,Department Of Computer Science And Business Systems,Chennai,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/icdsaai69492.2026.11505079","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,26]]},"container-title":["2026 International Conference on Data Science, Agents and Artificial Intelligence (ICDSAAI)"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.engappai.2023.107292","title":["Memory-efficient transformer network with feature fusion for breast tumor segmentation and classification task"],"author":[{"given":"Ahmed","family":"Iqbal","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Muhammad","family":"Sharif","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.engappai.2023.107292","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,1]]},"container-title":["Engineering Applications of Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.51358\/id.v22i3.1298","title":["Artificial Intelligence and Image Politics: Memory, Erasures, and Dissidences"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4812-5972","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Giselle","family":"Beiguelman","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.51358\/id.v22i3.1298","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,28]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>This paper analyzes the radical transformation of the visual regime brought about by generative artificial intelligence, characterizing it as a shift from photographic representation to statistical simulation. It argues that AI systems, by training on biased datasets, perpetuate a \"machinic eugenics of the gaze\" that reinforces racism, ageism, and gender norms. The concept of \"latent space\" is explored not merely as a technical domain, but as a site of tension between algorithmic erasure and the potential for dissident visualities. Through the discussion of the author's artistic research projects (specifically Botannica Tirannica and Poisonous, noxious and suspicious) and the case studies of Maria Bandeira and Luzia Pinta, the text demonstrates how AI can be used to fabulate archives for historically silenced narratives. Finally, it proposes a perspective rooted in the Global South that views technology as a tool for critical imagination and the activation of potential histories.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["InfoDesign"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/icdsaai65575.2025.11011613","title":["Artificial Intelligence in Alzheimer\u2019s Diagnosis: A Comparative Review of Machine Learning and Deep Learning Approaches"],"author":[{"given":"T.N","family":"Charanya","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Anna University,Chennai Institute of Technology,Department of Information Technology,Chennai,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Marimuthu","family":"A","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Anna University,Chennai Institute of Technology,Department of Information Technology,Chennai,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Nanditha","family":"S","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Anna University,Chennai Institute of Technology,Department of Information Technology,Chennai,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/icdsaai65575.2025.11011613","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,28]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,28]]},"container-title":["2025 International Conference on Data Science, Agents &amp;amp; Artificial Intelligence (ICDSAAI)"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.caeai.2026.100604","title":["Designing large language model-based agents with 5E framework for ESL learners\u2019 grammar acquisition"],"author":[{"given":"Xiaoxiao","family":"Yang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2248-5312","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xiaojing","family":"Weng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mengyao","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.caeai.2026.100604","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"container-title":["Computers and Education: Artificial Intelligence"]}],"items-per-page":100,"query":{"start-index":100,"search-terms":null}}}